1
00:00:00,300 --> 00:00:04,649
Your CPU runs code before it knows whether that code should run.

2
00:00:04,929 --> 00:00:06,844
Blame one tiny word: if.

3
00:00:07,594 --> 00:00:12,960
A core works like an assembly line, fetching new instructions while older ones finish.

4
00:00:13,240 --> 00:00:18,507
At an if, the line forks, and the answer isn't known until the comparison runs.

5
00:00:19,257 --> 00:00:25,634
So a branch predictor guesses from what that branch did recently, and the core races down the guessed path.

6
00:00:26,384 --> 00:00:28,464
Guess right, and no time is lost.

7
00:00:28,744 --> 00:00:35,169
Guess wrong, and that work is thrown out, the line is flushed, and fetching restarts on the correct path.

8
00:00:35,919 --> 00:00:41,531
On recent desktop chips, each wrong guess costs roughly fifteen to twenty clock cycles.

9
00:00:41,811 --> 00:00:44,934
That's why the same gigahertz can mean different speeds.

10
00:00:45,684 --> 00:00:50,217
The full assembly line is in our video, How a CPU Runs Your Code.

